{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Context\nOperator-approved (2026-08-12): make ticket-directory creation atomic so interrupted-write debris cannot occur. Evidence: fsck found 8 empty stray ticket dirs (MISSING_CREATE + FOREIGN_STORE_PATH), all the `makedirs`-then-die signature — the dir existed before its CREATE event file did, and interruptions (host sleep, process kill, timeout) in that window strand a plausible-looking empty ticket dir in the tracker working tree. Swept manually via tracker-maintenance --clean under ticket illsuited-erect-ibis (7c6e-4b8a-59c5-46db); the reducer's tolerance ruling (bug 043f: \"tolerate, never tidy\") deliberately rejects automatic cleanup of STORE DATA, so prevention at the writer is the self-healing fix.\n\n# Mechanism (confirmed against origin/main abf3817187)\n`_store/event_append.py::_prepare_event` runs `os.makedirs(ticket_dir, exist_ok=True)` at line 171, OUTSIDE the write lock; the first event file only lands at the `os.replace` in `stage_and_commit` (line 425), after the temp write, `write_lock` acquisition (30s x 2 attempts x retries) and the rebase guard. Death anywhere in that window leaves an empty ticket dir. fsck flags it TWICE: `fsck.py::_scan` Check 2 emits MISSING_CREATE (reduce yields None), and `fsck.py::foreign_store_path_list` emits FOREIGN_STORE_PATH (the dir holds no event file). Both scanners already skip dot-prefixed top-level entries, which is the naming convention this fix relies on.\n\n# Design\nNew leaf module `src/rebar/_store/staging.py` owning the convention and the atomic publish:\n- Convention: staging directories are `<tracker>/.tmp-newticket-<pid>-<uuid4hex>`. The leading dot is the EXISTING exclusion rule already honoured by `fsck._ticket_dirs`, `fsck.foreign_store_path_list`, fsck Check 1 and `relation_snapshot`, so temp paths are invisible to both checks with no scanner change. Documented in the module docstring and in docs/concurrency.md.\n- `stage_event(...)` -> `StagedEvent`: when the ticket dir does NOT exist, mkdir the staging dir and write the canonical event bytes INSIDE it; when it already exists, keep today's `.tmp-event-*` file staging unchanged.\n- `StagedEvent.promote()` runs UNDER the existing write lock (no change to lock discipline): re-checks the final path and either `os.rename(staging_dir, ticket_dir)` — one atomic step publishing dir+CREATE together — or, if a concurrent create won the race, `os.replace(staging_file, final_path)` + rmdir. The rename stays under the lock so an event file is never visible before `under_lock_check` (optimistic concurrency) has passed.\n- Failure/rollback paths remove the staging tree; a transaction that created the ticket dir and then rolls back also rmdirs the now-empty dir it just created, under the lock, within the same transaction.\n- `sweep_stale_staging(tracker)`: bounded, best-effort, called at ticket-create writer start. Removes ONLY `.tmp-newticket-*` staging dirs whose owner is provably gone, reusing `lock_owner`'s v2 ownership stamp (host + pid-namespace + process start time), which already defeats pid recycling; no stamp or an unprobeable one falls back to a conservative age guard. Bounded scan/removal caps.\n\n043f boundary: the sweep touches ONLY the writer's own staging area (`.tmp-newticket-*`), never a ticket directory and never store data. It does not reintroduce writer-side cleanup of event-less ticket dirs, which stays rejected; the code comment says so explicitly.\n\n# AC4 audit — other dir-then-first-file sites in the store write path\nConvert (same signature, same tracker, fsck-visible): `_engine/rebar_reconciler/inbound_translate.py::_write_event_file` (mkdir precedes `_seam.finalize_event`, which can raise CommandError on the secret screen, and lock acquisition, which can time out) and `_engine/rebar_reconciler/dispatch_one.py` BRIDGE_ALERT emission (mkdir then first alert file).\nExempt with reasons: `_store/lock.py::_acquire_mkdir` (dot-prefixed `.ticket-write.lock.d`, invisible to fsck, and its mkdir->stamp gap already has a staleness adjudicator); `_store/hlc.py` and `_commands/fsck_repair.py` marker dirs (under `.rebar/` or the resolved git dir, outside the committed worktree); `_commands/session_log.py`, `identity.py`, `trusted_env_cmd.py` pointer/config writers (not in the tracker at all).\n\n# Acceptance criteria\n- [ ] AC1 — Ticket creation stages dir+CREATE atomically: killing the writer at any point between staging start and rename leaves NO path that fsck reports as MISSING_CREATE/FOREIGN_STORE_PATH (temp paths are excluded from both checks by naming convention, documented).\n- [ ] AC2 — A RED test simulates the interruption (kill/exception injected between mkdir and event write in the OLD code shape) proving the debris signature, and the fix turns it green; a rename-crash test proves the temp path is invisible to fsck and cleaned on the next writer's start (bounded, best-effort temp sweep of stale temp paths owned by dead pids — this does not violate 043f since temp paths are the writer's own staging area, not store data).\n- [ ] AC3 — Concurrency: two concurrent creates of different tickets don't collide on temp paths (pid/uuid-suffixed staging names); the existing write-lock discipline is unchanged.\n- [ ] AC4 — Any other same-signature dir-then-file sites in the store write path are converted or explicitly recorded as exempt with reasons.\n- [ ] AC5 — make lint/typecheck pass; store suite green; no change to event formats or committed layout.\n\n# Testing\nNew `tests/interfaces/store/` test module:\n1. RED-shape reproduction: inject an exception at the point the OLD shape wrote the first event (patch `event_append` so staging fails after the dir would have been made), then assert `fsck.foreign_store_path_list` and the MISSING_CREATE scan report the ticket id — proving the debris signature exists; the converted writer makes the same injection leave zero fsck findings.\n2. Crash-before-rename: fail `os.replace`/the lock, assert the tracker holds a `.tmp-newticket-*` path, assert `foreign_store_path_list` and the fsck MISSING_CREATE scan both return nothing for it (invisibility by convention).\n3. Sweep: plant a staging dir stamped with a dead owner plus one stamped live; run a create; assert the dead one is gone and the live one untouched, and that no ticket directory was touched.\n4. Concurrency (AC3): two creates of different tickets in flight produce distinct staging names and both land.\n5. Byte parity unchanged: existing `test_canonical_event_bytes.py` / `test_store.py` residue assertions stay green.\nCommands: `make lint`, `make typecheck`, `python -m pytest tests/unit/test_store.py tests/unit/test_store_batch_write.py tests/interfaces/store tests/unit/test_plan_relation_snapshot.py`."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786584654872946001,"uuid":"fb9f4170-90ca-4e8c-8bd3-7aa6a93328bf"}